.TH RWY-FUSE 1 "" "Runway" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
rwy\-fuse \- mount a Runway store as a directory tree
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rwy\-fuse
[\fIfuse options\fR]
[\fB\-\-key\fR \fIpassphrase\fR] [\fB\-\-key\-file\fR \fIfile\fR] [\fB\-\-dek\-file\fR \fIfile\fR]
[\fB\-\-tpm\fR \fIdev\fR] [\fB\-\-freeze\-wait\fR \fIseconds\fR]
.I store
[\fIstore\fR...]
.I mountpoint
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rwy\-fuse
mounts a Runway store with FUSE.
It is a thin layer over the same core library that
.B RUNWAY.EFI
and the host tools use.
.PP
Each change made through the mount is one transaction.
It is durable when the system call returns: the new generation is published and flushed.
After a crash you see the old files or the new files, never a mix.
Renaming over an existing name is atomic.
.PP
The boot slots of
.I EFI/RUNWAY/
are also shown as a Boot Loader Specification view
.RI ( loader/entries ).
When the store has a
.B QUOTA
(see
.BR rwyctl (1)),
a new entry first removes the slots the quota asks for, in a commit of their own, as
.B rwyctl update
does.
The booted slot and the newest OK slot are never removed.
If the entry cannot fit, it is not stored and no slot is removed.
On an enrolled store, rwy-fuse checks the anti-rollback counter at mount and attaches it, so commits raise the epoch.
If the check fails, the mount works but no slot is removed (a warning is printed), and an entry that needs a removal is refused.
A refused entry is written to stderr and to syslog, because the commit follows the close of the file.
.PP
Writing
.B OK
to the
.B STATE
of the slot that booted, the health signal of the system, also deletes the
.B RunwayBootGuard
EFI variable, as
.B rwyctl confirm
does.
A failure is written to stderr and the write still succeeds.
.PP
Mount with
.B \-o default_permissions
so that the kernel checks the owner and the mode that the store keeps.
The core is single-threaded: run
.B rwy\-fuse
with
.BR \-s .
.PP
The store is locked.
When
.BR rwyctl (1)
runs against the mounted store, it connects to the daemon's RPC socket (see
.BR "RPC SOCKET" )
and the daemon executes the command in-process against its mounted store.
Without the socket it fails with exit status 5.
.PP
The mount source is
.BI rwy\-fuse: uuid
(the UUID of the store) and the file system type is
.BR fuse.rwy\-fuse .
.BR rwyctl (1)
reads the mount point of a store from
.IR /proc/self/mountinfo .
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I store
A file, a partition (such as
.I /dev/sda2
or
.BI PARTUUID= uuid\fR),
or
.IR image@offset [\fB+\fIlength\fR],
a store inside an image.
Give several stores to mount the members of one mirror.
Every member is locked.
The newest member is the primary; a member that is behind is left out.
A write that cannot reach at least one member fails, and
.B rwy\-fuse
warns when it runs degraded.
.TP
.I mountpoint
An existing directory.
.SH OPTIONS
Options that
.B rwy\-fuse
does not know are given to FUSE.
Useful ones are
.BR \-s ,
.BR \-f ,
.B \-o allow_other
and
.BR "\-o default_permissions" .
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " \fIpassphrase\fR"
Passphrase of an encrypted store.
It is visible in the process list; prefer
.BR \-\-key\-file .
.TP
.BI \-\-key\-file " file"
.TQ
.BI \-\-passphrase\-file " file"
Read the passphrase from a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-dek\-file " file"
Read the data key (32 raw bytes) from a file.
.TP
.BI \-\-tpm " dev"
TPM device or Unix socket used to unseal the key (default
.IR /dev/tpmrm0 ).
.TP
.BI \-\-freeze\-wait " seconds"
How long the requests wait during a freeze (default 120).
After that they fail with
.B EAGAIN
until the thaw.
0 fails them at once.
.PP
The key sources are, in order: the options, the variables
.B RWY_KEY
and
.BR RWY_DEK_FILE ,
systemd credentials
.RB ( $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY ),
and the TPM.
This lets
.I boot.mount
mount an encrypted store with nobody present.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B RWY_KEY
Passphrase of an encrypted store.
.TP
.B RWY_DEK_FILE
File with the data key.
.TP
.B RWY_TPM
TPM device, if
.B \-\-tpm
is not given.
.TP
.B RWY_RUN_DIR
Directory of the freeze socket (default
.BR /run/rwy\-fuse ).
.TP
.B RWY_CRYPTO
Set to
.B builtin
to use the built-in cipher code instead of OpenSSL.
At start, before the mount is served, the daemon removes the files that a killed
.BR rwyctl (1)
left in
.I /.staging/
(no
.B rwyctl
can be copying yet).
.SH RPC SOCKET
After the mount, the daemon listens on a Unix socket named
.IB uuid .sock
(the UUID of the store) in the directory
.BR /run/rwy\-fuse ,
or in
.B RWY_RUN_DIR
if it is set.
The socket has mode 0600 and only the user of the daemon (root, for the boot mount) may connect.
It is removed when the daemon ends and replaced if a killed daemon left it behind.
If the socket cannot be made, the daemon warns and goes on without it.
.PP
When
.BR rwyctl (1)
connects to the socket, it sends its command arguments together with open file descriptors for standard output,
standard error, and any input files over
.BR SCM_RIGHTS .
The daemon flushes any buffered writes, runs the command in-process on its mounted store, invalidates its
directory and BLS caches after mutations, and replies with the exit code.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.RS
rwy\-fuse \-s \-o default_permissions /efi/RUNWAY.DAT /boot
.RE
.PP
A mirror:
.PP
.RS
rwy\-fuse \-s a.store b.store /mnt
.RE
.PP
Unmount with
.BR fusermount3 " " \-u .
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mount.runway (8),
.BR rwyctl (1),
.BR fsck.runway (8)
